    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nDocumented `CmdAtom` based dot-repeat doesn't work with `!` built-in operator in some situations where regular dot-repeat does.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create a `init.lua` with `CmdAtom` based dot-repeat (as of 1deeff9c3898) along with repro lines:\n\n    ```lua\n    local last ---@type vim.event.cmdatom.data?\n    vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('CmdAtom', {\n      callback = function(ev)\n        if ev.data.changed and ev.data.lhs ~= '.' then\n          last = ev.data\n        end\n      end,\n    })\n    vim.keymap.set('n', '.', function()\n      -- CmdAtom is deferred; schedule the replay, in case \".\" follows an edit.\n      vim.schedule(function()\n        if last then\n          vim.api.nvim_feedkeys(last.keys or last.lhs, last.keys and 'n' or 'm', false)\n        end\n      end)\n    end)\n\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { 'b', 'a', '', 'e', 'c', 'd' })\n    ```\n\n2. `nvim --clean -u init.lua`\n3. Type `!` - `ip` - `sort` - `<CR>`. It sorts the first two lines after executing the expression `.,.+1!sort`.\n4. Type `3j` to move to the start of the next paragraph.\n5. Press `.`. It sort the first two lines of the paragraph and not the whole paragraph. Using the regular `.` sorts the whole paragraph\n\nThis is because the last (and only) `CmdAtom` event after the first sorting hard-codes the `keys` with `.,.+1` range, which will not be recomputed on the `.`. The full `data` of the first `CmdAtom` event:\n\n```lua\n{\n    changed = true,\n    cmd = \":\",\n    keys = \":.,.+1!sort\\n\",\n    lhs = \":.,.+1!sort\\n\",\n    moved = false,\n    pos = { 1, 0 },\n    text = \".,.+1!sort\",\n    type = \"excmd\",\n    undoseq = 2\n}\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nDocumented recipe for `CmdAtom` based dot-repeat works in all cases where the regular dot-repeat works (otherwise it is not a strict improvement).\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1389+gd3b4f562a6\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo, doesn't have this functionality\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.1.9-arch1-2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage and from source",
